<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    The Go GC write-barrier diamond (docs/multilang.md W2/W5), ported as kuna
    `option gowritebarrier` (P2 flow-classification, default AUTO).

    Every pointer store the garbage collector must see compiles to a flag
    test: when `runtime.writeBarrier` is set, call a `runtime.gcWriteBarrier<N>`
    thunk (which hands back the GC pointer buffer, R11 on amd64), record the
    old and new values in that buffer, then rejoin the hot path - and BOTH
    paths perform the program's own store at the join.  The cold arm is GC
    bookkeeping, and poison on top of noise: the thunk's buffer register is
    not a modeled return, so the recording stores read an undefined local.

    Stage model: the shared guarded-cold-call shape
    (`infra/idiom.rs::match_guarded_cold_call`) - a one-block cold arm holding
    exactly one call, whose guard rejoins the hot path in one hop - with the
    callee held to the `gcWriteBarrier` family, cut with the
    `securitycheck`/`stackguard` primitives (`Funcdata::removeBranch` +
    `removeUnreachableBlocks`) in the PRE-SSA window, directly after
    `gomorestack`.

    The bytes are a hand-assembled x86-64 guarded store, exactly the shape the
    Go compiler emits (polarity included: the cold arm is the fall-through):

        100000  test rdi,rdi            ; the writeBarrier flag test
        100003  jz   100010             ; barrier off -> straight to the store
        100005  call 101000             ; runtime.gcWriteBarrier2
        10000a  mov  [r11],rsi          ; record the value in the GC buffer
        10000d  nop  dword [rax]
        100010  mov  [rdi],rsi          ; the program's own store (the join)
        100013  ret

    P0 assertion: `option gowritebarrier off|auto|always`.  `auto` (the
    default) acts only on an image whose detected source language is Go; the
    analyzer tier never runs on this bytechunk bootstrap, so the test forces
    `always`, exactly as the `gomorestack` and `rustabi` stage tests do.
    Pass 1 (option gowritebarrier off): the bug - the guard renders as an `if`
      whose arm calls the thunk and writes the buffer (assertions #1, #2, and
      the third `= a1;` of #3).
    Pass 2 (option gowritebarrier always): the fix - the `if`, the call and
      the buffer write are gone; the program's own store appears once per pass
      (assertion #4), never deleted and never duplicated.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
4885ff740be8f60f00004989330f1f00488937c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="store"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x101000" name="runtime.gcWriteBarrier2"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>option gowritebarrier off</com>
  <com>lo fu store</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option gowritebarrier always</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="GOWRITEBARRIER #1: the thunk call renders under off and folds under always" min="1" max="1">gcWriteBarrier2\(</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="GOWRITEBARRIER #2: the flag guard renders under off and folds under always" min="1" max="1">if \(</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="GOWRITEBARRIER #3: off carries the buffer write plus both real stores" min="3" max="3">= a1;</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="GOWRITEBARRIER #4: the program's own store survives both passes, once each" min="2" max="2">\*a0 = a1;</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
